A||||23||wardog85GT||Does blocking off your EGR on a V6 really work?||||5||
Z||000000||wardog85GT||12-04-2001||07:36 PM||||Well does it? My 85 GT could use some more power. How much work is it?||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||12-04-2001||08:12 PM||||I made a blockoff plate of .090-thick 7075 aluminum sheet (because I have some).  <BR>To answer your 2nd question: It wasn't hard to do.  Used it to cure a fast idle (the EGR tube was cracked).  As soon as I got a replacement EGR tube, I took the blocker off.||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||12-04-2001||09:05 PM||||Well, considering it closes at wide open throttle, the answer is no. But of the EGR stuff is all goofed up, the answer is yes, it works to cover up the symptoms.<P>More power? No.||||reg||1
Z||000003||lou_dias||12-04-2001||09:34 PM||||I just cut up and aluminum can because I had one.<P>terryk, I respect you and your opinions.  In theory it may not work but too many people who have done it will tell you otherwise.<BR>I admit I only got a slight gain at first and some mild hunting but 3 months later, it's smoother and my engine SCREAMS!  105k on the odo...  I think the computer has finally adjusted full is the only way I can explain it.  Maybe it's the reformulated gas.  I'm using 89 octane...  All I know it that it kicks...hard baby...||||reg||1
Z||000004||recluse||12-04-2001||10:23 PM||||I was wondering about this also.  Does unplugging the vaccum line that goes to the EGR and pulgging it give the same effect as blocking it off the EGR completely?  I tried this and my car seemed to have a little less power.  Maybe it was just my imagination, but it sure did'nt have more.||||reg||5
Z||000005||FieroPerformance||12-04-2001||10:26 PM||||I had a cracked tube so I blocked it off. I used gasket material from a left over exhaust manifold gasket and aluminum to block each hole off. I passed E-Check the next day.<P>I noticed a HUGE power increase.... but I think that is because I rebuilt the engine, put in a new cam, high compression power forged pistons, and that the engine had 5 busted compression rings when I took it apart.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>-Charlie<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/newsigfperformance.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||87GTZ34||12-04-2001||10:31 PM||||Folks lets think about what Terry said.  If the EGR is closed off (not re-circulating exhaust) at WOT then why would blocking it off yield more power?   The only explanation would be that the EGR system had a leak in the first place.  If the car is under hard acceleration or at WOT, then the A/F mix is enrichened (burns cooler) and doesn't need EGR to 'cool the combustion chamber' so the EGR is not needed.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fierowrecker||12-04-2001||11:06 PM||||I love this topic!!!<BR>The EGR, developed in the sixties and federally mandated in the seventies...<BR>Dilutes the air fuel ratio with a nonburnable (preburnt) gasses to cool the combustion temps in order to lower nitrogen oxide emmissions...<BR>Also lowers the efficiency of the engine and causes higher fuel useage, so, in order to bring up the mileage, "lean burn" is introduced...<BR>Heat up the air intake, and lower the amount fuel required to run the engine...<BR>But this causes detonation or "pinging" under certain conditions, so lower the compression ratio so it doesn't die with a death rattle...<BR>But lowering the compression ratio also lowers the efficiency of the engine and causes higher fuel useage to try and make up for the lost performance...<BR>So now we will add turbo chargers in order to gain back the lost performance, but they only work at mid to high RPM's, while gear ratios are chosen for low RPM's to keep milage acceptable...<BR>BAA HUMBUG!!!!<BR>A properly built and maintained high performance engine is no dirtier than an emmission ecno-engine of today...<BR>The only reason we see ancient technology on our cars is due to the fact it is federally mandated...<BR>And getting the laws changed for the better is near impossible...<BR>A 150ci engine putting out 3HP per cubic inch would compaire to a 300ci engine getting 1HP per ci...<BR>And the 150 would have less NO emmissions and better mileage than the 300ci engine......||||reg||1
Z||000008||West Coast Fiero||12-04-2001||11:07 PM||||well, when folks are doing this modification, are they remebering to jump terminals C and D in the EGR solenoid pigtail? Or do they just live with the constant engine light?<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000009||MR2KLR||12-04-2001||11:22 PM||||I thought if you blocked off he EGR you would promote detonation. Is that right? I would also think that by NOT useing a EGR system of any kind you would get a "more pure" fuel mix in each cyl. makeing more power.<P>Would this make more power for free? what about on a 88 coupe? (4cyl)<P>------------------<BR>  <BR>Black 88 Coupe: 120 MPH spedo, Aux. gauge pod, 195/55VR14 Potenza RE-71 tires, GT exhaust & ground F/X Soon to have 16" White Beretta GTZ W/ 215/45ZR16's<BR><A HREF="http://community-2.webtv.net/TOMTOM81/MYNEWTOY/" TARGET=_blank>Tom's Page</A>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Master_Sushi||12-05-2001||12:47 AM||||Ya, has anyone tried this on a duke? Will it do anything for performance? I allready had my cat "fall off" so im not worried about emmissions.||||reg||5
Z||000011||lou_dias||12-05-2001||07:57 AM||||and EGR is built around the same concept for any engine.  I didn't block off my vaccuum tube, my light doesn't come on and I grounded out nothing.<P>my light came on SOMETIMES on the highway for the first week and that was it.  3 months later running great and strong.  I think most people get a little scared at first when the car is a little jumpy from 1000-1800 rpms, but other than that it's fine.<P>Have you guys really looked at and studied that UPOS (u-useless), I've replace 3 of those because they are inherently junk.  A little vaccumm tube is supposed to lift a fairly stiff spring, I don't buy it.  If you lift that spring all the way up your engine chokes and stalls.  Too much hot, uncombustable air and not enough freash air get into your engine.  This is supposed to close at WOT how?  It's driven by vaccuum, sure there is less vaccuum at WOT but at higher RPMs you'll build vacuum again and the valve opens.<P>It just limits HP to make the tree-huggers happy.  Sure the right thing to do is also get a custom chip but for the $$$ and the fact that the computer will adjust a bit over time, it's not worth it.<P>This is a free mod just like punching out the air silencer.||||reg||1
Z||000012||johnt671||12-05-2001||11:04 AM||||I did it on my duke and the only diff I felt was a little bit better throttle response.  Not sure if that applies because after I plugged it I changed the gasket under the throttle body I found the passages plugged up, so the valve never was working.  I have tried plugging it since I cleaned the passages but maybe I will just to try it again.  ||||reg||1
Z||000013||falconhulk||12-05-2001||11:05 AM||||Lous Pulling the vac line to the EGR and plugging it is much easier and works just the same by preventing the EGR from opening.  You get no light as the sensor is on the solenoid not the actual egr tube.||||reg||1
Z||000014||lou_dias||12-05-2001||12:13 PM||||well, my egr dangles from the cross-over, it's cracked at the base but somehow it was woring enough to crack the tube and melt some vaccuum tubes and distributer cap.<BR>when I replace the cap and tubes, that's when I finally blocked it off and left the vaccuum on.  I think my mechanic set my timing to 8 degrees instead of 10 at that point.||||reg||1
Z||000015||MinnGreenGT||12-05-2001||12:26 PM||||OK... as far as I'm reading (please correct me if I'm wrong):<P>There are 2 ways to disable the EGR... block off the entire tube at the base by installing a blocking-plate.  Or just remove the vaccum line from the EGR solinoid and plug the vaccum line.  Either one may cause the "service engine" light (which I ocassionally get due to a faulty EGR anyway).  I could also bypass the EGR sensor by jumping terminals C&D in the EGR solinoid pigtail (where is this anyway).<P>Is this right?  Thanks!<P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||falconhulk||12-05-2001||12:30 PM||||Plugging the vac line will not throw a code.  As far as the computer knows the EGR is functioning.  Please be aware that a lot of people on this fourm think that disableing the EGR can lead to head damage.  They say that it casues the engine to run hotter even though the guage does not register it.  i think Orief did a long explanation in a thread a few months ago.||||reg||1
Z||000017||MinnGreenGT||12-05-2001||12:33 PM||||Although I'm guessing that just plugging the line will not do the job <B>if</B> you have a cracked EGR tube (it'll still suck air).  Right?||||reg||1
Z||000018||mrfixit58||12-05-2001||02:53 PM||||I think if your EGR tube is cracked you'll create a vacuum leak.<P><P>------------------<BR>Roy  :D <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR>Suncoast Fieros<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG">          <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <P><B>JUST ADDED: </B>1988 GT w/ T-Tops, 5-Speed, and Performance Sound.||||reg||4
Z||000019||Formula88||12-05-2001||03:03 PM||||Well, from what I've heard, blocking off the EGR at the plenum will cure the vacuum leak of a broken EGR pipe, but could raise cylinder temps - not necessarily engine coolant temps.  If cylinder temperature (combustion temperature) goes too high, you can burn a piston.  I believe Ed Parks has said he's seen lots of engines with blocked off EGR's and burnt pistons.  You may not notice it right away, but it's accelerating the wear on your engine.  Perhaps running Premium fuel will keep the combustion temperature cool enough?  <P>Just know you've been warned.  Do this at your own risk.  I blocked off mine when I had a broken EGR pipe.  I noticed no performance improvement, other than fixing a vacuum leak.  When I got a new pipe, it took off the block.||||reg||4
Z||000020||1FST2M6||12-05-2001||04:22 PM||||it makes no dyno-able HP/TQ difference.. it will raise your combustion temps and do a few things.. fells like faster throttle responce @ part throttle to part throttle, (not sure if its psychological or really does) it does give you worse fuel mileage and in very extreme cases can burn a hole in a piston or burn an exhaust valve. but i run no egr just cause i broke the plastic line and didn't want to fool with it. but i've dynoed my cars many many times and it makes no difference at WOT.  ||||reg||1
Z||000021||00lE||12-05-2001||06:45 PM||||Within the GM ECM code found in the Fiero there is a table such as this one :<P>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/egr_table.jpg">       <P>The function of this table is to <B>lean out</B> the fuel mixture by changing the injector pulse width.  It does not matter if you block off the egr, the mixture will still be leaned out, since the values used are determined by "desired egr" rather than "detected egr" and the values will still be used just as if the egr was functioning properly (unless an egr related  code has been set).  <P>While the egr is functioning as it should, this leaning of the mixture is fine since the cylinder mixture has been diluted by the inert egr gases and therefore requires less fuel to obtain the correct ratio.  If the egr has been disabled without changing the appropriate tables (there are other EGR related tables for timing, etc) the engine will eventually be damaged from the detonation which occurs due to an overly lean mixture.  While this fact is less important on an engine system that incorporates a knock (i.e. detonation) sensor, the Fiero V6 does not use one, and therefore the potential for damage is exceptionally high.<P>Can you hear the damaging knock that would occur if you disable the egr without changing the code?  Very unlikely, unless you are riding in the engine compartment  with a stethoscope (one of the reasons GM uses a sensor threaded into the block to detect it.)<P><p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 12-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||DRH||12-05-2001||07:24 PM||||Very good explanation 00le.<P>Below is a link to an article with a similar explanation. It mentions that the timing is also advanced based on requested EGR, another way to cause knock.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.mindspring.com/~fierosketch/osg/EGR.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mindspring.com/~fierosketch/osg/EGR.html</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000023||West Coast Fiero||12-05-2001||07:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MinnGreenGT:<BR><B>OK... as far as I'm reading (please correct me if I'm wrong):<P>There are 2 ways to disable the EGR... block off the entire tube at the base by installing a blocking-plate.  Or just remove the vaccum line from the EGR solinoid and plug the vaccum line.  Either one may cause the "service engine" light (which I ocassionally get due to a faulty EGR anyway).  I could also bypass the EGR sensor by jumping terminals C&D in the EGR solinoid pigtail (where is this anyway).<P>Is this right?  Thanks!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The 4 wire connector that plugs into the EGR solenoid will be marked A,B,C,D. You want to remain the connector plugged in, however with it plugged in you want the wires which go to C and D to be jumped ( or shorted ) just before the connector. This will enable the EGR system to be dissabled, independent of the computer. Be ABSOLUTELY sure that you do not jump wires A and B other wise you will be sending 12V directly to the computer therby frying it<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
